      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the smallest number of coins \\( x \\) that satisfies both conditions:\n1. \\( x \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6} \\)\n2. \\( x \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\)\n\nWe will use the Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT) to solve this system of congruences. However, we can also solve it using the method of successive substitutions or by checking possible values of \\( x \\).\n\nFirst, let's list the common multiples of 6 and 5:\n- Multiples of 6: \\( 6, 12, 18, 24, 30, 36, \\ldots \\)\n- Multiples of 5: \\( 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30, \\ldots \\)\n\nWe need to find a common multiple of both 6 and 5. The least common multiple (LCM) of 6 and 5 is 30. So, we start by checking multiples of 30:\n- \\( 30 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{6} \\) (which doesn't satisfy \\( x \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6} \\))\n- \\( 60 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{6} \\) (which doesn't satisfy \\( x \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6} \\))\n\nNext, we check multiples of 30 plus the differences to get the value we need:\n- \\( 30 + 6 = 36 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{6} \\)\n- \\( 36 + 6 = 42 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{6} \\)\n- \\( 42 + 6 = 48 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{6} \\)\n- \\( 48 + 6 = 54 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{6} \\)\n- \\( 54 + 6 = 60 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{6} \\)\n\nNext, we check multiples of 30 minus the differences to get the value we need:\n- \\( 30 - 5 = 25 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\)\n- \\( 25 - 5 = 20 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{5} \\) (which doesn't satisfy \\( x \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6} \\))\n- \\( 20 - 5 = 15 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{5} \\) (which doesn't satisfy \\( x \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6} \\))\n\nFinally, we check multiples of 30 plus the differences to get the value we need:\n- \\( 30 + 3 = 33 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\)\n- \\( 33 + 3 = 36 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{6} \\) (which satisfies \\( x \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6} \\))\n\nWe also check if \\( 36 \\) is a valid solution for the second condition:\n- \\( 36 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\) (which doesn't satisfy \\( x \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\))\n\nSo, we need to find a common solution. Let's test some values around 36 and see if any of them satisfy both conditions:\n- \\( 35 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{6} \\) (not relevant here)\n- \\( 34 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{6} \\) (not relevant here)\n- \\( 33 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\) and \\( 33 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6} \\) (solution found)\n\nSo, the smallest \\( x \\) that satisfies both conditions is 33. We need to find the remainder of 33 when divided by 7:\n\\[ 33 \\div 7 = 4 \\text{ remainder } 5 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of coins left when equally divided among seven people is \\( \\boxed{5} \\).",
